data.content:
Here's what happened:

**C# 14 span overload resolution change** — In .NET 10, new built-in span conversions cause `array.Contains(num)` inside an expression tree to bind to `MemoryExtensions.Contains(ReadOnlySpan<T>, T)` instead of `Enumerable.Contains(IEnumerable<T>, T)`. Expression trees cannot represent span-based methods, so this throws at runtime.

**Fix** — Force the call to bind to `Enumerable.Contains` by casting to `IEnumerable<T>`:

```csharp
// Option 1: Cast
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => ((IEnumerable<int>)array).Contains(num);

// Option 2: AsEnumerable()
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => array.AsEnumerable().Contains(num);

// Option 3: Explicit static call
Expression<Func<int[], int, bool>> expr =
    (array, num) => Enumerable.Contains(array, num);
```

Any of these prevents the compiler from choosing the span overload.
